 ],
 "literal": "Even if the hill owl hoots, in the front yard / even if the male monkey leaps and frolics on the dark branches of the jackfruit tree, / my heart fears, alas, pitiable now indeed, / in the deep dark night, towards him, / on the long path of the mountain slope, his journey does not cease.",
 "translation": "Even when the hill owl hoots, or the male monkey leaps and frolics on the dark branches of the jackfruit tree in our front yard, my poor heart is filled with fear now. For in this deep, dark night, his journey along the long path of the mountain slope towards me does not cease.",
 "modern": "மலைக் கூகை அலறினாலும், முன்றிலில் உள்ள பலா மரத்தின் கரிய கிளையில் ஆண் குரங்கு பாய்ந்து குதித்தாலும், என் நெஞ்சம் இப்போது மிகவும் அஞ்சி இரங்கத்தக்கதாக ஆகிறது. ஏனெனில், இந்த அரிய இருள் நிறைந்த இரவில், மலைச் சாரலின் நீண்ட வழியில் என்னை நோக்கி அவர் வரும் பயணம் இன்னும் ஓயவில்லை.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her confidante about her intense anxiety regarding the hero's nightly journeys to meet her. The poem is set in the Kuriñci landscape, characterized by mountains, jackfruit trees, and wild animals. Even minor, natural occurrences in her own front yard—such as the hooting of a hill owl or the sudden rustling of a male monkey leaping on a jackfruit branch—startle her and fill her with dread. This psychological state reflects her constant terror over the physical dangers the hero faces while traveling along the long, dark mountain slopes in the dead of night. The key phrases are 'āriruṭ kaṅkul' (deep dark night) and 'celavāṉātē' (his journey does not cease), which emphasize the peril of his path.",
